{"answer":"\\((x^2-4)/(x-2)=x+2\\) for \\(x\\ne2\\). The original domain is \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{2\\}\\). It is not equivalent to \\(x+2\\) at every real number, because the original expression is undefined at \\(x=2\\), whereas \\(x+2=4\\) there.","approaches":[{"description":"Factor the numerator as a difference of squares and cancel the common nonzero factor on the original domain.","id":"factor-cancel","outcome":"candidate","reason":"This directly determines both the simplification and the excluded input."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"question_premise","basis_reference":"The question restricts attention to “real x where the expression is defined.”","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"defined-scope","kind":"assumption","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The requested simplification concerns real inputs at which the original quotient is defined.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in step-factor and step-cancel.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["defined-scope"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"simplified-form","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"For every real x with x ≠ 2, (x^2 - 4)/(x - 2) = x + 2.","step_ids":["step-factor","step-cancel"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in step-domain.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"original-domain","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The domain of the original expression is R \\ {2}.","step_ids":["step-domain"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in step-nonequivalence using the original-domain and simplified-form claims.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["original-domain","simplified-form"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"not-global-equivalence","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The original and simplified expressions are not equivalent at every real number.","step_ids":["step-nonequivalence"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"step-factor","justification":"Difference of squares: x^2 - 4 = (x - 2)(x + 2).","statement":"Rewrite the numerator as (x - 2)(x + 2)."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-factor"],"id":"step-cancel","justification":"When x ≠ 2, the factor x - 2 is nonzero and may be cancelled.","statement":"For x ≠ 2, ((x - 2)(x + 2))/(x - 2) = x + 2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"step-domain","justification":"A real quotient is defined precisely when its denominator is nonzero; here x - 2 ≠ 0 exactly when x ≠ 2.","statement":"The original expression is defined exactly for x ∈ R \\ {2}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-domain","step-cancel"],"id":"step-nonequivalence","justification":"At x = 2 the original quotient has denominator zero and is undefined, while x + 2 is defined and equals 4.","statement":"The equality after cancellation holds only on the original domain, not at every real number."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
